# SEO

## Core rule

SEO is part of the content architecture, not a layer added at the end.

## Titles and descriptions

Every indexable page should have:
- unique title
- useful meta description
- canonical URL
- appropriate Open Graph metadata

Titles should describe the actual page, not repeat the company name unnecessarily.

## Heading structure

Use one clear H1 per page.
Use H2/H3 according to information hierarchy.

Do not create headings for styling purposes only.

## Product SEO

Product pages should have:
- descriptive product names
- stable slugs
- unique descriptions
- relevant internal links
- useful image alt text
- structured data when appropriate

Avoid thin product pages created only to target keywords.

## Target topic language

Use natural language around relevant commercial topics such as:
- exotic vegetables
- exotic vegetable supplier
- premium fresh produce
- exotic vegetables for restaurants
- specialty vegetables
- microgreens supplier
- fresh produce supplier
- B2B fresh produce supplier

Do not keyword-stuff.

## URLs

Use short, stable, human-readable URLs.
Avoid:
- numeric IDs
- query-string-only identity
- unnecessary nested levels

## Internal links

Create purposeful links between:
- products and categories
- products and journal content
- who-we-serve pages and relevant products
- sustainability content and about/company content
- product pages and quote CTA

## Technical SEO

Provide where applicable:
- XML sitemap
- robots.txt
- canonical URLs
- Open Graph metadata
- favicon/site identity
- image alt text
- structured data

Use structured data only when the content genuinely supports it.
Do not fabricate review, price, stock, or rating data.

## Indexing

Do not accidentally block the main site from indexing in production.
Preview/staging behavior should be explicit.

## Performance

SEO and performance are linked.
Prioritize:
- fast hero rendering
- optimized images
- minimal client JavaScript
- stable layout
- crawlable content
